Subject: register VM DRC reconcile on allocation pressure and taken back-edges

What this does:
  Deferred reference counting (DRC) parks zero-refcount values until a
  safepoint reconciles them. The safepoint trigger was purely a count
  (zct_len + zct_nobj > zct_limit), so a handful of very large parked values
  - e.g. multi-megabyte linearized strings, which pdf.js produces constantly -
  kept the heap ratcheting up without ever tripping the limit. The monolith
  register VM also had no safepoint on its taken back-edges at all, so a long
  running function could accumulate parked values for its whole duration.

  This adds three triggers and the missing back-edge check:
  1. rt->drc_malloc_mark: set after every reconcile (and after JS_RunGC's
     reconcile) to malloc_size + max(4 MB, 25%). js_malloc_rt / js_realloc_rt
     call js_drc_malloc_pressure(), which sets zct_limit = 0 when allocation
     has crossed the mark and the parked list is non-empty. The reconcile
     itself still happens at the next safepoint, never inside malloc.
  2. An eager trigger in js_drc_zero(): parking a single large value (string or
     rope >= 64 KB, comparable BigInt, or an object owning a large array buffer
     / typed array) sets zct_limit = 0 directly.
  3. js_trigger_gc()'s deferred branch sets zct_limit = 0 when values are
     parked, preferring a reconcile over merely raising the collection
     threshold.
  4. REGVM_MONO_BACKEDGE(off): the monolith register VM now calls
     js_drc_safepoint() on every taken backward branch, matching the threaded
     handlers' REGVM_DRC_BACKEDGE.

Why we need it:
  On the Octane pdf.js corpus the register VM peaked at 1.46 GB RSS versus
  23 MB for the stack VM (and 57.5 MB after this change, versus 58.4 MB for a
  QJS_DRC=0 build), while the live heap at exit was smaller than the stack
  VM's. It was a transient peak, not a leak: parked values were retained far
  past their last use.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Runtime only.

Notes:
  The generated step-engine include quickjs-regvm-step.inc is regenerated so
  its copy of REGVM_CMP_BRANCH carries the new back-edge check (it is emitted
  from the monolith body). The only change there is the added
  REGVM_MONO_BACKEDGE(off) at the taken-branch sites; it keeps the shipped
  include in sync and avoids a macro-redefinition warning.

